Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Bastion**, a huge fortified keep whose solar
collector crown rotates, whose gate raises and lowers, and whose beacon slowly
turns, as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You
build it by **painting discrete opaque cells** and you **author its idle
animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Bastion is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you sculpt,
how you break the keep into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you
shape every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a keep whose
crown spins, gate raises, and beacon sweeps actually needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json`
— together with the rig you define are your submission.

You paint **discrete opaque cells**: `set-voxel`/`fill-box` and the other cell
operations lay down solid `#rrggbb` material — there is no transparency and no
smoothing, just crisp voxel forms. Every voxel operation targets a **part** with
`--part <name>`; each part is sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log,
so create a part before you sculpt into it. The volume starts **empty**. Each call only
records the operation to that part's log and renders **nothing** — run `voxel-anim
render` to (re)draw the previews and the assembled scene (`--component <part>`
redraws one part; `--time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at an
instant of an animation), and you **must** run `voxel-anim render` before you finish
so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry the run is scored from — an unrendered part
is scored as empty.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving
each a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author
the required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Each part is a
separate volume of cells composited in the shared volume's coordinates, so position
each part where it belongs on the finished keep.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name keep
voxel-anim fill-box --part keep --x0 1 --y0 0 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 5 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.)
Run `voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands
(defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations as
F-curves with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `parts/<part>.png` after you run `voxel-anim render`** to see that part's progress,
**and read the assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`,
`scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole bastion composed
from all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you
confirm the parts actually fit together: that the crown rings the summit, the gate
sits in the front wall, and the beacon stands atop the spire. Because each part is
sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on
the finished keep; the scene simply composes them there.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build and rig the bastion up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a massive masonry keep with thick ramparts,
corner towers, and a central spire, a collector crown ringing the summit, a gate in
the front wall, and a beacon atop the spire — with a clear solar-amber energy
accent. **Author the three required animations** as F-curves with the `voxel-anim`
animation subcommands: `crown_spin` (the crown turning a full revolution on its
own), `gate_raise` (the gate lifting straight up, holding open, and lowering), and
`beacon_spin` (the beacon turning slowly on its own) — all self-playing idles that
run with no caller while the keep stays fixed. The animations are the fixed contract
— produce all three, by those names; the parts and joints that realize them are
yours. When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are
your finished submission.
